      {
        "id": "p6-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that $a$, $b$ and $c$ are in arithmetic progression if and only if\n$$b=\\frac{a+c}{2}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$b-a=c-b$ if and only if $2b=a+c$, which is equivalent to $b=\\frac{a+c}{2}$.",
          "worked": "Three numbers are in arithmetic progression exactly when their consecutive differences are equal.\n$$\\begin{align*}b-a&=c-b\\\\2b&=a+c\\\\b&=\\frac{a+c}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nEach step is reversible, so the condition is both necessary and sufficient.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q15-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "An arithmetic sequence has an odd number of terms. Prove that the middle term is the average of the first and last term.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $2k-1$ terms, $\\frac{a_1+a_{2k-1}}{2}=a_1+(k-1)d=a_k$, the middle term.\n\nUse the arithmetic-sequence formula for the last and middle terms.",
          "worked": "Let there be $2k-1$ terms with common difference $d$. The middle term is $a_k$, and the last term is $a_{2k-1}=a_1+(2k-2)d$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{a_1+a_{2k-1}}{2}&=\\frac{a_1+a_1+(2k-2)d}{2}\\\\\\frac{a_1+a_{2k-1}}{2}&=\\frac{2a_1+2(k-1)d}{2}\\\\\\frac{a_1+a_{2k-1}}{2}&=a_1+(k-1)d\\\\\\frac{a_1+a_{2k-1}}{2}&=a_k.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the middle term is the average of the first and last terms.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
